body.push(H1("5. Results"));
body.push(H2("5.1 Reading the results"));
body.push(P("Sections 5.2 to 5.7 report the original outputs of the five-seed matrix, produced before this revision (Section 4.8), with five seeds (17 to 21), three attack settings, six arms, and five rounds, which gives 450 rows. Sections 5.8 and 5.9 report the 20-seed extension, run in the reproduction environment, and Section 5.10 reports the perturbation measurements. For seeds 17 to 21, the extension's values for the shared arms come from the rerun and can differ slightly from Sections 5.2 to 5.7, so the two sets are not mixed within any table. In Sections 5.2 to 5.7, unless stated otherwise, numbers are percentages and are means over the five seeds, shown with the half-width of a 95% Student t interval (4 degrees of freedom). Contrasts between arms are seed-paired differences, reported with the same kind of interval and with the number of seeds in which the first arm had the lower value. Because the same five seeds underlie every comparison, and because no hypothesis tests or multiplicity corrections were applied, the intervals describe the spread of this pilot and are not tests of superiority. The primary view uses the arm-specific validation-calibrated thresholds of Section 4.6. Test FPR at those thresholds ranged from 13.22% to 17.02% across the 90 round-5 seed, arm, and attack runs, and validation FPR ranged from 4.99% to 5.00%, so the 5% target holds on validation data and not on the test partition. The common fixed threshold is a secondary view, reported in Section 5.5."));
body.push(P("The BIM-like attack gave round-5 attack-induced ASR within 0.1 percentage points of the FGSM-like attack for every arm (for example, 4.41% against 4.49% for the static arm). Tables 7 and 8 report all three settings. Tables 9 and 10 report the FGSM-like and decision-only settings. The BIM-like clean-side values, including clean F1, are in the analysis files, and they agree with the FGSM-like values to within 0.02 points in every arm mean."));

body.push(H2("5.2 Attack-induced ASR at round 5"));
body.push(P("Table 7 reports attack-induced ASR after round 5, which is the share of flows that an arm detects before perturbation and that the attack then moves to the benign side. Figure 2 shows the same quantity with the five individual seed values."));
