body.push(P("Clean F1 is reported in Tables 9 and 10 beside the other clean-side measures (Section 4.7). Its values are identical across the FGSM-like and decision-only settings for arms A and F, whose models do not depend on the attack, and for arm B, because its decision-only evaluation uses the same FGSM-like static examples. For arms C, D, and E the models differ between settings, because each update uses the evasions found in that setting. In the BIM-like setting, the static examples come from the five-step attack, so arm B's clean F1 also differs slightly from its FGSM-like value. Clean F1 depends only on a model and its threshold, so equal models give equal values and differing models give differing values. The stored values were recalculated from clean recall, clean FPR, and the test class counts (45,332 malicious and 37,000 benign), and they agree to rounding in all 450 rows. Their arm means differ by at most 0.53 points at calibrated thresholds, where arm D has 86.78% in the FGSM-like setting and 87.31% in the decision-only setting, and by at most 0.31 points at the fixed threshold. Per-seed differences for arm D reach 1.94 points at calibrated thresholds. The BIM-like clean-side values agree with the FGSM-like values to within 0.02 points in every arm mean, and to within 0.06 points for any single seed. In these runs the round-5 outcomes of the two gradient settings were also nearly identical, and their label counts were identical for every arm and seed. We report this as an observed result, and Section 6.5 discusses it. For its five shared arms, the earlier final matrix in the experiment bundle, run with the same protocol and seeds, matches these values (Section 4.8). At calibrated thresholds, clean F1 is 89.06% for the static arm and 89.11% for the control, against 88.49% for the frozen arm and 86.78% for the no-rehearsal arm, which also has the lowest clean recall."));
