body.push(P("Table 10 and Figure 3 show why the fixed-threshold view alone can mislead. At the fixed threshold, arm C reached 5.02% attack-induced ASR in the FGSM-like setting, which looks close to the static arm's 4.01%, but its FPR was 20.93% against 15.34% for the frozen arm, an increase of 5.59 points (interval 0.92 to 10.27, higher in every seed). Arm D reached 0.00% attack-induced ASR with an FPR of 43.70%, which means its model flags a large share of benign flows at that threshold. Arm E moved the other way, with FPR of 5.50%, recall of 83.85%, and the highest attack-induced ASR (11.66%). Once each arm receives its own validation threshold (the filled markers), the arms C, D, and E all fall back near the frozen arm's FPR and their attack-induced ASR values move to within a few points of it. This is the pattern that motivated the calibrated analysis. The static arm and the control moved little between thresholds, though the static arm's fixed-threshold FPR varied widely across seeds (half-width 6.06 points), which suggests that its threshold behavior is less stable than its calibrated behavior. The reduced fixed-threshold ASR of arm C is therefore not evidence of robustness at a matched operating point, since part of it comes with more false alarms."));

