body.push(P("Table 11 shows three things. First, the incremental arms received few labels, with five-seed means between about 21 and 111 over five rounds, because only a small share of the 100 flows attacked each round evaded at the fixed threshold. Label counts differ across arms because each arm's own model produces its own evasions, so the label budgets are not matched, and the static arm used 500 pre-built examples that are not counted as post-deployment labels. Second, the five-seed mean of adaptation-stage queries, summed over five rounds, ranged from 20,324 (arm E) to 24,017 (arm D) of a possible 25,000, because every arm is attacked each round whether or not it updates, and so queries do not separate the policies. Third, time differs little in absolute terms. The static arm and the control took about twice as long as the frozen arm (0.88 s against 0.44 s in the FGSM-like and BIM-like settings, and 1.20 s against 0.75 s in the decision-only setting), which is consistent with their five extra training epochs. The run metadata record a static-arm update time close to the initial training time in every run. Relative to the frozen arm, the incremental arms differ by between −0.012 and +0.034 seconds in the means over five seeds. The largest difference is arm D in the decision-only setting, at 0.787 seconds against 0.753 seconds. These differences combine update training with attack generation against each arm's model, which the records do not separate, so they are small relative to the attack-generation cost in the decision-only setting and are not read as differences in policy cost. These timings come from a small NumPy model on a single machine and should not be extrapolated to larger detectors or to real analyst effort, which this study did not measure."));


