body.push(P("For RQ5, the comparison between the one-time and incremental schedules depended on how the one-time arm was trained. In both gradient settings, the matched all-attempt incremental schedule (M2) had lower observed attack-induced ASR than the one-time arm with a single two-epoch update (M1) in 19 of 20 seeds, by 1.17 points in the FGSM-like setting (95% interval 0.80 to 1.53). The one-time arm with a single ten-epoch update (M1-e10) had lower observed attack-induced ASR than M2 in all 20 seeds, by 1.58 points (1.20 to 1.96). The one-time arm that matched M2's update calls, optimizer steps, and per-vector exposure (M1-seq) differed from M2 by 0.01 points (−0.02 to 0.04). Its round-5 attack-induced ASR equalled M2's in 15 of 20 seeds, although the two arms' thresholds and clean-side values differed slightly in every seed, so their models were not identical. M1 was higher than M1-seq, which uses the same vectors, by 1.16 points in 19 of 20 seeds, which is almost the whole M1 − M2 difference. In the decision-only setting none of the matched arms separated, and every interval for their differences includes zero."));
body.push(P("In these runs, the difference between M1 and M2 therefore goes with the update structure and total exposure, and not with whether the attempts were generated against a model that changes across rounds. Once update calls, optimizer steps, and exposure were matched, generating the vectors against the current model (M2) or against the initial model (M1-seq) made no visible difference to round-5 attack-induced ASR. This holds for this detector, this attack budget, and round 5. M1-seq completes all its updates before round 1, so it is not a like-for-like comparison with M2 in earlier rounds, and the extension does not show that either scheduling policy is generally superior."));
body.push(P("The matched arms kept their clean-side behavior. In the FGSM-like setting, M1, M1-e10, M1-seq, and M2 had clean recall between 88.79% and 88.95%, test FPR between 15.02% and 15.08%, AUC of 0.959 to 0.960, and pre-attack detection of 98.9% to 99.0%, against 89.06%, 15.08%, 0.960, and 100% for the frozen arm. Their post-attack benign rates over the whole cohort were 10.37%, 7.60%, 9.14%, and 9.13%. The decision-only setting gave similar clean-side values. Lower attack-induced ASR in the matched arms therefore did not come with a visible loss of sensitivity or a rise in false alarms. M2 also had lower observed attack-induced ASR than the rehearsal arm C in 18 of 20 seeds in both gradient settings, by about 0.7 points. The two arms share the clean memory and the rule within each update, but M2 trains on all 100 attempts in each round, while C trains only on its successful evasions, so they are different update policies."));
body.push(P("The original static arm had lower observed attack-induced ASR than M1, which trains on the same number of attack attempts, in every seed, by 3.99 points in the FGSM-like setting and 2.82 points in the decision-only setting. The two arms differ in their source flows (initial-training flows against adaptation flows), in the clean data trained with them (the full initial training part for five epochs, against a 1,000-example memory for two), and therefore in the total amount of training. The static arm's lower observed ASR thus goes with its overall training procedure and not with one-time scheduling as such, and these runs do not identify which part of that procedure matters. In the decision-only setting, the static arm trained on FGSM-like examples had lower observed attack-induced ASR than B-dec, trained on decision-only examples, by 1.32 points (1.14 to 1.50) in all 20 seeds. Replacing the cross-attack baseline with a threat-model-matched one would therefore have raised, not lowered, the static arm's observed ASR in that setting. The cross-attack caveat still applies to the original five-seed comparison. B-dec's clean recall and FPR (90.64% and 15.60%) were close to the static arm's."));

body.push(H2("5.9 Sensitivity to the update settings"));
body.push(P("Table 14 reports the pre-declared sensitivity analysis in the FGSM-like setting over the same 20 seeds. Every configuration is shown, and none was selected. Changing the update epochs, learning rate, or clean-memory size moved the round-5 attack-induced ASR of the updating arms (from 7.80% to 8.97% for C, 8.10% to 9.67% for M1, 5.33% to 7.74% for M1-e10, and 7.44% to 8.68% for M2), but it did not change the order of M1, M1-e10, and M2. In all seven configurations, M2 had lower observed attack-induced ASR than M1 in 15 to 19 of 20 seeds (mean differences 0.60 to 2.13 points), and M1-e10 had lower observed attack-induced ASR than M2 in 19 or 20 of 20 seeds (mean differences −0.86 to −2.21 points). The rehearsal arm C was lower than the frozen arm in 11 to 17 of 20 seeds, depending on the configuration, with mean differences from −0.14 to −1.31 points. Across all configurations and these arms, clean recall stayed between 88.52% and 89.07% and test FPR between 14.65% and 15.12%. The analysis covers only the FGSM-like setting, varies one factor at a time, and does not include M1-seq. It shows how stable the ordering of M1, M1-e10, and M2 was around the default settings in that setting. It does not establish that ordering for the BIM-like or decision-only attacks, and it does not identify the best settings."));
body.push(caption("**Table 14.** Pre-declared sensitivity of round-5 attack-induced ASR (%) to update settings, FGSM-like setting, 20 seeds, validation-calibrated thresholds. Arm values are mean ± 95% t half-width. The last two columns give the mean seed-paired difference and the number of seeds (of 20) in which M2 was lower than M1, and in which M1-e10 was lower than M2.", true));
