body.push(P("Table 15 shows that, for most perturbed features, the bound allows changes far larger than the typical value in the cohort, because each range spans the most extreme values in the initial training part. Two relations hold in the data and the attack does not keep them. The official feature list defines tcprtt as the sum of synack and ackdat [36], and this held in every training record and every cohort flow before perturbation. After the FGSM-like and BIM-like attacks it was broken in every perturbed flow, and after the decision-only attack in every flow that the attack changed. The rate feature equals (spkts + dpkts − 1) / dur in every training record with positive duration and in 96.3% to 97.2% of cohort flows. After the gradient attacks it was broken in 75.3% to 98.4% of perturbed flows across seeds, and after the decision-only attack in 85.2% to 92.7% of changed flows. Between 70.1% and 73.7% of each cohort are non-TCP flows, all of which have zero TCP timing values before the attack. Every perturbed non-TCP flow received nonzero TCP timing values. Most perturbed vectors therefore do not describe flows of the kind found in the data, and the attack-induced ASR values in this paper measure sensitivity to unconstrained feature perturbations, not evasion with traffic that could be sent."));

body.push(H2("5.11 Summary against the research questions"));
body.push(P("For RQ1, the static arm had lower observed attack-induced ASR than the frozen arm in every seed and all three settings (by 3.31, 3.43, and 2.21 points), while the incremental arms with rehearsal and balanced replay showed differences from the frozen arm whose intervals include zero. Static training also had lower observed attack-induced ASR than incremental training with rehearsal in every seed and every setting. The no-rehearsal arm showed a lower attack-induced ASR but with wide intervals and a loss of sensitivity. For RQ2, the static arm's clean-side shift (slightly higher recall and FPR) was shared with the control. The no-rehearsal arm lost recall and AUC at the calibrated threshold, and the fixed-threshold view exposed large FPR changes for arms C, D, and E that the calibrated view does not show. For RQ3, the control had lower observed attack-induced ASR than the frozen arm in every seed, by 0.96 points in the FGSM-like setting and 0.95 points in the decision-only setting. The static arm had lower observed attack-induced ASR than the control in every seed, by 2.35 and 1.27 points in those two settings. The two arms differ in their adversarial examples, so the comparison is descriptive, and it does not divide the static arm's difference between extra training and adversarial examples. For RQ4, the incremental arms received five-seed means of about 21 to 111 simulated labels over five rounds, and the static arm and the control took about twice the wall-clock time of the frozen arm. Five-seed mean adaptation-stage queries, summed over five rounds, ranged from 20,324 (arm E) to 24,017 (arm D), out of 25,000 possible. These findings are exploratory. They come from five seeds, one dataset, one small model, feature-space attacks, and simulated labels, and in the decision-only evaluation the static arm was trained on FGSM-like examples. They do not show that any policy is superior, and Section 6 discusses how far they can be interpreted."));
body.push(P("Over 20 seeds in the extension runs, the static arm again had lower observed attack-induced ASR than the frozen, rehearsal, and control arms in every seed of every setting. For RQ5, with 500 attack attempts on the same source flows for every matched arm, the result depended on how the one-time arm was trained. In the gradient settings, M2 was lower than M1 (one two-epoch update) in 19 of 20 seeds, M1-e10 (one ten-epoch update) was lower than M2 in all 20 seeds, and M1-seq, which matched M2\'s optimizer steps and exposure, did not differ from M2. In the decision-only setting the matched arms did not separate. In the FGSM-like sensitivity analysis, the order of M1, M1-e10, and M2 was the same in all seven update configurations. The original static arm stayed lower than M1 in every seed, and in the decision-only setting the threat-model-matched static arm had higher observed attack-induced ASR than the FGSM-trained one. Most perturbed flows break relations that hold in the data (Section 5.10)."));

// ===== Section 6 =====
body.push(H1("6. Discussion and Limitations"));
body.push(H2("6.1 What the results show"));
body.push(P("The study asked whether repeated updating from newly observed evasions gives a detector more lasting resistance than a single round of adversarial training. In this pilot, the data did not show that it does. At validation-calibrated thresholds, static adversarial training had lower observed round-5 attack-induced ASR than the frozen model in every seed and every attack setting (RQ1), and its observed ASR was also lower than that of incremental training with rehearsal in every seed and setting. These observed differences are not attributed to the update strategy alone, because the static arm and the incremental arms receive unmatched training-example budgets. The incremental arms with rehearsal and balanced replay stayed close to the frozen model, with paired intervals that include zero, and their per-round trajectories showed no sustained decline (Figure 4). We therefore describe incremental updating as showing no consistent advantage over the frozen model here. This statement is limited to the configuration studied. It is not a finding that incremental updating cannot help."));
body.push(P("The clean-continued control gives context for the static result (RQ3). With extra clean epochs, the control had lower attack-induced ASR than the frozen model in every seed, by 0.96 points in the FGSM-like setting and 0.95 points in the decision-only setting. The static arm was lower than the control in every seed in both settings, by 2.35 and 1.27 points. The two arms differ in their 500 adversarial examples, so this comparison is descriptive. It shows where the two arms differ, but it does not divide the static arm's difference between extra training and adversarial examples. Without the control, the whole static-versus-frozen difference could be read as an effect of the adversarial examples. The control gives a descriptive check on that reading under the tested configuration. Because the arms differ in their training data and budgets, it does not isolate the causal effect of either the adversarial examples or the extra training."));
body.push(P("The matched-attempt extension (RQ5) changes how the main result should be read. When one-time and incremental schedules trained on the same 500 attack attempts from the same source flows, under the same rule within each update, their order depended on how the one-time arm was trained. A short one-time update gave higher observed attack-induced ASR than the matched all-attempt incremental schedule, a longer one gave lower, and a one-time arm that matched the incremental schedule's update calls, optimizer steps, and exposure gave the same, in the gradient settings. The decision-only setting showed no separation. In these runs, the differences between the matched arms therefore track training structure and exposure, and generating the attempts against a changing model added nothing visible at round 5. The original static arm, which trains for five more epochs on the full initial training part together with its examples, stayed lower than M1 in every seed. Its lower observed ASR in the main comparison goes with that training procedure, and the evidence does not support reading it as an advantage of one-time over incremental scheduling. In the FGSM-like sensitivity analysis, the order of M1, M1-e10, and M2 did not depend on the default update settings. That analysis did not cover the other attack settings or M1-seq."));

body.push(H2("6.2 Possible explanations"));
body.push(P("The main comparison was not designed to isolate why the arms differ. The extension tested one candidate explanation, and the rest remain hypotheses. First, the policies of the main comparison receive very different amounts of adversarial data. The static arm trains on 500 pre-built adversarial examples, whereas the incremental arms accumulated five-seed means of about 21 to 111 simulated labels over five rounds, because only a small share of the 100 flows attacked in each round evaded the current model at the fixed threshold. Second, the matrix evaluated one configuration per update policy. The update epochs, learning rates, and memory sizes were not varied in this matrix, so we cannot tell whether the incremental updates were too small to move the model, and other settings could change the picture. This differs from the validation-based threshold calibration, which sets operating points on validation data and is not a search over update settings. Third, the static arm receives its adversarial examples in a single batch before evaluation, while the incremental arms receive theirs in small amounts over time, so the policies differ in timing as well as in quantity. The extension matched the number and source-flow set of attack attempts for one-time and incremental arms, and the addendum arm M1-seq also matched update calls, optimizer steps, and exposure. With exposure matched, the one-time and incremental arms did not differ at round 5, while changing the one-time arm's update structure with the same vectors (M1 against M1-seq) moved attack-induced ASR by about 1.2 points. This points to training structure and exposure, and not the timing or target model of the attempts, as the factor that separated the matched arms in these runs. The mechanism by which exposure lowers attack-induced ASR was not tested."));
body.push(P("One explanation for the near-identity of the two gradient settings is untested. At ε = 0.03, the gradient sign may change little along the five steps, in which case five steps of size ε/5 would move each feature by about the same total amount as one step of size ε in the same direction. The experiment did not record gradient signs along the attack path, so this explanation is a hypothesis and not a finding."));
body.push(P("The no-rehearsal arm illustrates the forgetting concern in [26]. Its update batches contain malicious examples only, and it lost sensitivity (clean recall 85.35% and pre-attack detection 93.66% in the FGSM-like setting) while its AUC fell. This is consistent with the argument for rehearsal in [27], but our design does not test the mechanism, and the loss could also reflect the class composition of the update batches. The balanced-replay arm shows a different pattern. At the common fixed threshold, arm E had lower test FPR and lower recall than the frozen arm (FPR 5.50% against 15.34% and recall 83.85% against 89.28% in the FGSM-like setting). At its validation-calibrated threshold, its recall and FPR were close to the frozen arm's (89.30% and 15.48% against 89.28% and 15.34%), and both arms had an AUC of 0.961. These aggregate measures show that the observed classification behavior depends on the threshold. They do not show how the score distributions changed, because they were not analyzed."));

body.push(H2("6.3 Lessons for measurement"));
body.push(P("Three measurement points follow from the results and apply beyond this pilot. First, a threshold held fixed across update policies can mislead, because updating changes how a model scores flows. At the common fixed threshold, arm C reached an attack-induced ASR of 5.02%, close to the static arm's 4.01%, but its test FPR was 5.59 points above the frozen model's, and arm D reached 0.00% with an FPR of 43.70% (Table 10, Figure 3). Under validation-calibrated thresholds, these differences largely disappeared. Reporting attack-induced ASR without the matching false-positive rate would have favored arms that simply flagged more traffic. This is consistent with the general caution in [29] that evaluation choices can change the conclusions of security studies."));
body.push(P("Second, attack-induced ASR and the post-attack benign rate answer different questions and should not be merged. The first conditions on flows the arm detected before perturbation. The second covers the whole evaluation cohort and therefore includes flows already classified as benign before the attack. For arm D, the lower attack-induced ASR came with lower pre-attack detection, and we do not interpret the higher post-attack benign rate as evidence of more attack-induced evasions. Third, the calibrated and fixed views differ in what they hold constant. The calibrated view holds validation FPR near 5% and lets test FPR vary (13.22% to 17.02% across the 90 round-5 runs), so it is not an equal-operating-point comparison on the test set. We chose it because it avoids tuning on test data, and we report test FPR so that readers can judge the trade-off."));

body.push(H2("6.4 Relation to prior work"));
body.push(P("Our results are not directly comparable with earlier work on dynamic NIDS. Study [13] reports that continuous retraining can reduce attack effectiveness even without adversarial training, whereas in this pilot the incremental arms with rehearsal and balanced replay did not separate from the frozen model. The two settings differ in several ways, including bounded feedback-driven updates, a per-round attacker budget, a one-time adversarial baseline, and a clean-continued control, and this study cannot establish which of these differences accounts for the apparently different outcome. The defenses reviewed in Section 2.3 report robustness gains from adversarial training and related methods, and the static arm's lower attack-induced ASR is consistent in direction with that body of work. The gradient attacks we use follow [18] and [19] and the decision-only search follows the hard-label setting of [22], so the comparison with the literature on those attacks is limited to the feature-space threat model."));

body.push(H2("6.5 Limitations"));
body.push(P("Table 16 lists the main limitations and how each affects interpretation. The results should be read within these limits."));
body.push(caption("**Table 16.** Main limitations and their effect on interpretation.", true));
